> I don't actually know what's good for these intrinsics, all I'm doing is helping flesh out the docs.

Due to the UB check, the following code cannot be transformed to `unchecked_add`:

```rust
pub unsafe fn foo(x: i32, y: i32) -> i32 {
    x.checked_add(y).unwrap_unchecked()
}
```

After eliminating `UbChecks`, the resulting MIR is as follows:

```
    bb0: {
        StorageLive(_3);
        StorageLive(_5);
        StorageLive(_6);
        StorageLive(_7);
        _7 = CheckedAdd(_1, _2);
        _5 = (_7.0: i32);
        _6 = (_7.1: bool);
        StorageDead(_7);
        StorageLive(_4);
        _4 = unlikely(move _6) -> [return: bb1, unwind unreachable];
    }

    bb1: {
        _8 = Eq(_4, const false);
        assume(move _8);
        goto -> bb3;
    }
```

At this point, we can use `assume` to transform `CheckedAdd` into `AddUnchecked`.

https://github.com/rust-lang/hashbrown/issues/509 is the corresponding real-world example.

I don't think the perf result is very concerning. The tests here clearly demonstrate the change works, so this looks like the usual MIR inliner chaotic effect: We made some functions smaller so now the MIR inliner makes different decisions and some of those are profitable for compile time and some aren't. @rustbot label +perf-regression-triaged

I think there's interesting follow-up work to do here with tweaking the order this transform is applied, I think it might be better before UnreachablePropagation, but that change could result in messy perf reports so I'm happy landing this as-is.

